   311	#### 4.2.1 Portable `manager-worker-v1` execution policy
   312	
   313	Every source-aware `go-v1` and `go-repository-v1` operation runs under exactly
   314	one named execution policy. Protocol 1.0 defines exactly one, the portable
   315	`manager-worker-v1` policy, and every conforming manager MUST implement it on
   316	macOS and Windows. The policy identity is a normative cache, receipt, marker,
   317	and claim input. It is never a package-visible option, a host label, or an
   318	operator preference.
   319	
   320	The fixed process graph is:
   321	
   322	```text
   323	manager parent
   324	  -> identity-verified manager-owned worker
   325	       -> fingerprinted <GOROOT>/bin/go
   326	            -> fingerprinted regular executables below
   327	               <GOROOT>/pkg/tool/<GOHOSTOS>_<GOHOSTARCH>/
   328	```
   329	
   330	The worker is an exact re-execution of the installed manager executable in one
   331	fixed hidden mode. It is an implementation boundary, not a user-visible command
   332	surface and not a package-selected program. No package file, manifest value,
   333	descriptor value, environment value, `PATH` lookup, shell, or user option
   334	selects the worker executable or its mode. An implementation that cannot
   335	distribute an equivalent identity-verified worker MUST state the exact
   336	executable graph it verifies instead and MUST treat every mutable component of
   337	that graph, including an interpreter and an installed package tree, as trusted
   338	computing base.
   339	
   340	One worker session performs exactly one `go list`, waits while the parent
   341	validates the complete package graph, accepts exactly one authenticated build
   342	permit, and performs exactly one `go build`. The session state machine admits
   343	no retry, second list, second build, additional executable, shell, VCS, module
   344	download, generator, test, run, or tool request; any other message tears the
   345	session down without starting a compiler.
   346	
   347	The following controls are mandatory on every supported host. A manager that
   348	cannot apply all of them MUST reject the build before starting the worker or Go:
   349	
   350	- the fixed offline vendored-Go behavior, argument vectors, environment, and
   351	  canonical working directories defined above;
   352	- operation-private user, configuration, cache, temporary, staging, and output
   353	  roots resolved independently of package data;
   354	- a manager-derived artifact path, a bounded wall-clock deadline over the whole
   355	  worker domain, bounded and redacted combined output, and one bounded regular
   356	  artifact;
   357	- closed standard input and release of unrelated descriptors or handles before
   358	  Go starts;
   359	- worker executable identity verification before launch, an in-session identity
   360	  proof bound to a fresh session nonce, and re-verification of the worker and
   361	  fingerprinted toolchain identity after the last child exits and before
   362	  publication;
   363	- termination and joining of the complete worker domain before the operation
   364	  returns; and
   365	- every native containment or resource control the host actually provides,
   366	  including descendant-domain termination and the active-process, aggregate
   367	  memory, per-file size, and inherited-handle controls where the platform
   368	  exposes them.
   369	
   370	The portable policy does not provide, and a conforming implementation MUST NOT
   371	claim, these hardened guarantees: total network denial; kernel-enforced
   372	read-only source and toolchain; kernel-enforced private-build-root-only writes;
   373	hard aggregate descendant process, memory, disk, time, and output bounds; exact
   374	executable allowlisting; and fail-closed preflight of those capabilities. They
   375	are specified separately by the hardened execution profile tracked as
   376	`STORY-260728-327soo`. The absence of a hardened capability MUST NOT reject a
   377	portable build, and a portable operation MUST NOT record it as applied.
   378	
   379	Host capability evidence records, per operation, which native controls the host
   380	provided and which were unavailable. It is reporting state. A manager MUST NOT
   381	report an unavailable control as applied, MUST NOT emit a hardened guarantee
   382	under `manager-worker-v1`, and MUST NOT place capability evidence in a cache
   383	key, receipt input, marker record, or claim, because portable cache identity is
   384	defined by the mandatory controls that every conforming host applies.
   385	
