   191	#### 2.2.1 Portable `manager-worker-v1` worker session
   192	
   193	The manager implements exactly the portable execution policy of Protocol Core
   194	section 4.2.1 and applies it identically to `go-v1` and `go-repository-v1` on
   195	macOS and Windows. The worker is one hidden-mode re-execution of the installed
   196	manager executable. In order, one operation:
   197	
   198	1. runs the package-independent toolchain probes and freezes the validated
   199	   source snapshot;
   200	2. resolves its own executable to a canonical regular installed file, rejects
   201	   symlink, reparse-point, and hard-link substitution, records strong file
   202	   identity, and hashes the bytes;
   203	3. opens anonymous inherited pipes or handles and launches exactly that
   204	   executable in the fixed hidden worker mode, rechecking identity at the launch
   205	   boundary so a replacement race cannot widen the graph;
   206	4. sends one length-bounded canonical request carrying a fresh session nonce,
   207	   the expected executable identity, the canonical working directory, the fixed
   208	   environment, the private roots, the applicable limits, the exact Go and tool
   209	   identities, and both permitted Go argument vectors;
   210	5. requires the worker to prove the same executable identity and hash, release
   211	   unrelated descriptors or handles, close standard input, apply every mandatory
   212	   and every available native control, and acknowledge the nonce together with
   213	   its applied-control evidence;
   214	6. runs exactly the fixed `go list` vector and returns bounded output and exit
   215	   metadata; the worker cannot proceed to a build on its own;
   216	7. applies every dependency, containment, directive, and native-input rejection
   217	   of section 2.3 to the complete stream;
   218	8. sends exactly one authenticated fixed build permit and runs exactly the fixed
   219	   `go build` vector, tearing the session down on any other message;
   220	9. returns one bounded regular artifact through manager-controlled private
   221	   staging, applies manager-defined permissions, and verifies type, size, link
   222	   safety, identity, and digest without executing it; and
   223	10. re-verifies the worker and fingerprinted toolchain identity, then terminates
   224	    and joins the complete worker domain and discards all private state before
   225	    publication.
   226	
   227	A failure at any step fails the operation before publication. A pre-launch
   228	identity, substitution, or mandatory-control failure fails before the worker
   229	starts. A protocol, nonce, ordering, message-size, message-kind, or
   230	permit-ordering failure fails before the compiler starts.
   231	
   232	The manager records host capability evidence naming the native controls the host
   233	provided and those it did not. That evidence is reporting state only. The
   234	manager MUST NOT report an unavailable control as applied, MUST NOT present any
   235	deferred hardened guarantee as satisfied under `manager-worker-v1`, and MUST NOT
   236	place capability evidence into a cache key, receipt input, marker record, or
   237	conformance claim. A missing hardened capability does not reject a portable
   238	build; a missing mandatory control does.
   239	
   240	Execution-boundary results use these stable `phase: execution` diagnostics,
   241	which apply to both compiled drivers and take precedence over the generic
   242	schema and descriptor codes of section 11.10:
   243	
   244	| Code | State | Severity | Meaning |
   245	|---|---|---|---|
   246	| `build_execution_worker_identity_invalid` | `blocked` | `error` | Worker or toolchain executable identity, substitution, or replacement check fails |
   247	| `build_execution_worker_protocol_invalid` | `blocked` | `error` | Session framing, nonce, ordering, size, message kind, or permit sequence is invalid |
   248	| `build_execution_control_unavailable` | `unsupported` | `error` | A mandatory portable control cannot be applied on this host |
   249	| `build_execution_capability_evidence_invalid` | `corrupt` | `error` | Capability evidence reports an unavailable control as applied or contradicts the applied profile |
   250	| `build_execution_hardened_claim_forbidden` | `unsupported` | `error` | A deferred hardened guarantee is claimed under the portable execution policy |
   251	| `build_execution_package_influence_forbidden` | `unsupported` | `error` | Package data attempts to influence the execution boundary, worker, controls, limits, permits, or publication |
   252	
   253	### 2.3 Dependency and artifact preflight
   254	
   255	On a real cache miss, the fixed `go list` command MUST complete before the
    72	requires a new closed identifier and independent security review.
    73	
    74	`go-v1` forces internal linking and disables libgcc lookup, cgo, PGO,
    75	workspaces, toolchain switching, non-standard assembly, host objects, and the
    76	active non-standard `//go:cgo_import_dynamic` directive. If the native target
    77	or dependency graph cannot build within that surface, installation fails. A
    78	manager MUST reject a build when it cannot enforce the fixed environment,
    79	offline dependency policy, source/context separation, native target, and
    80	process graph; it MUST NOT approximate the contract.
    81	
    82	### Portable execution boundary
    83	
    84	Compiled builds run under exactly one named execution policy. Protocol 1.0
    85	defines only the portable `manager-worker-v1` policy of `protocol/core.md`
    86	section 4.2.1. It adds one identity-verified manager-owned worker between the
    87	manager parent and the fingerprinted Go toolchain so that every mandatory
    88	control is installed before any package byte reaches a compiler.
    89	
    90	The trusted computing base of that boundary is the installed manager parent and
    91	worker bytes; the worker framing, authentication, and session state machine; the
    92	capability probe and control adapters; the operating-system primitives those
    93	adapters use; the fingerprinted Go binary and `GOROOT` tools; source and
    94	build-root canonicalization, fingerprinting, and input validation; the
    95	operation-private roots and artifact verifier; and the policy, cache, and
    96	receipt canonicalization code. An implementation that launches the worker
    97	through a mutable interpreter or installed package tree adds those to the same
    98	trusted base and MUST say so.
    99	
   100	The added threats are worker identity substitution and replacement races,
   101	session framing and replay attacks, out-of-order or duplicated build permits,
   102	and dishonest capability evidence. The contract answers them with pre-launch
   103	identity verification, an in-session identity proof bound to a fresh nonce,
   104	identity re-verification after the last child exits, a state machine that admits
   105	exactly one `go list` and one `go build`, and full worker-domain teardown before
   106	publication.
   107	
   108	Package-controlled bytes cannot select or modify the worker executable or its
   109	hidden mode, the Go or tool executable paths, any argument vector, environment
   110	value, working directory, tag, or flag, the applied controls, limits, or
   111	permitted roots, the worker messages or the parent's build permit, or the
   112	graph-validation result, artifact verifier, cache key, receipt, marker, claim,
   113	publication, or artifact execution.
   114	
   115	The portable policy is honest about its limits. It does not provide total
   116	network denial, kernel-enforced read-only source and toolchain, kernel-enforced
   117	private-build-root-only writes, hard aggregate descendant process/memory/disk/
   118	time/output bounds, exact executable allowlisting, or fail-closed preflight of
   119	those capabilities. Those six guarantees belong to the separately tracked
   120	hardened execution profile (`STORY-260728-327soo`) and MUST NOT be claimed by a
   121	portable build. Their absence does not reject a portable build; falsely
   122	recording them does. Because the execution-policy identity is inside the hashed
   123	build input, a portable artifact, receipt, marker, and claim can never be
   124	mistaken for hardened output, and host capability evidence never enters cache
   125	identity.
   126	
   127	### Compiler-input trust boundary
   128	
   129	Compile-only does not mean trusted source or an invulnerable compiler. Malicious
   130	source may exploit parser/compiler defects or exhaust CPU, memory, disk,
   131	processes, diagnostic output, or filesystem traversal. The selected Go
   132	toolchain, manager, operating system, hashing/canonicalization code, and
   133	manager-owned policy/staging roots are trusted; package source and compiler
   134	diagnostics are not. Source is read-only to children, standard input is closed,
   135	and stdout/stderr MUST be bounded and redacted before presentation. Under the
   136	portable execution policy, managers MUST apply the mandatory controls of
   137	`protocol/core.md` section 4.2.1 plus every native filesystem, network, time,
   138	memory, disk, process-count, and output control the host actually provides, and
   139	MUST record the rest as unavailable rather than as applied. Bounding a
